hadiths[1]{hadithnumber,text}:
"2947","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Aban, who narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Abi Adiyy, on the authority of Humayd al-Tawil, on the authority of Bakr ibn Abdullah. And narrated to us Abu Bishr al-Wasiti, who narrated to us Khalid, on the authority of Humayd, on the authority of Bakr. This is the hadith reported by Ibn Abi Adiyy:\nA Bedouin came to the Saqaya (water station) and drank nabidh. He then said: \What is the matter with the people of this household that they serve nabidh"","" while their paternal cousins serve milk and honey? Is this out of stinginess or need?\"" Ibn Abbas (RA) – and this occurred after he had lost his sight – said: \""Bring the man to me.\"" The man was brought to him"","" and Ibn Abbas (RA) said: \""This is not due to stinginess or need on our part. Rather"","" the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) entered the mosque while mounted on his camel"","" with Usama ibn Zayd behind him. He asked for a drink"","" so we served him nabidh"","" which he drank. He then offered the remainder to Usama"","" and said: 'You have done well and beautifully"","" so do likewise.' We have no intention of changing this practice.\""\nAbu Bakr (RA) then said: \""This report falls under the same category of narrations we record in our books: Indeed"","" Allah"","" the Mighty and Majestic"","" permits a matter with a general mention"","" and clarifies in another verse on the tongue of His Prophet (ﷺ) that what He permitted with a general mention"","" He intended only a portion of that matter which He mentioned generally"","" not all of it. Likewise"","" the Prophet (ﷺ) permits a matter with a general mention"","" and clarifies at a later time that what he mentioned generally"","" he intended only a portion of that matter"","" not all of it. For example"","" His saying: 'Eat and drink until the white thread becomes distinct to you from the black thread' [Quran 2:187]. He gave a general mention in this verse of what is eaten and drunk"","" and clarified in another context that He only permitted part of what is eaten and part of what is drunk"","" not all of it. This is a lengthy chapter which I have explained in detail in other places in my books. Thus"","" the Prophet (ﷺ) only permitted drinking from the nabidh of the Saqaya when it is not intoxicating"","" as he knew that intoxicants are forbidden.\""\n---"""""
